  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal temperatures will continue through most \nof the upcoming week, with a few showers possible at times mid-week.\n\nSurface high pressure over Ontario will build into the eastern Great \nLakes region today. Waves of low pressure will move along a surface \ntrough from the Ohio Valley to the Mid-Atlantic region through \ntonight. Drier air will continue to spread from north to south \nacross the forecast area. Regional radar shows rain approaching from \nsouthern Michigan to eastern Ohio this afternoon. As the high builds \nin from the north, rain is expected to dive southeast into western \nPennsylvania through this evening. A few widely scattered showers \ncan't be ruled out across the western Southern Tier, however \nmostly dry weather is expected across the forecast area. \n\nAn elongated area of high pressure across Ontario and Quebec \nProvinces will persist through much of the work week. An easterly \nflow will support cool weather with daytime highs in the 60s, 50s \nacross the higher terrain. Monday and Tuesday are expected to be the \ncoolest days of the week. Weak troughing to zonal flow will set-up \nacross the region with a closed low over the mid-west through the \nweek. Subsidence and dry air will maintain mostly dry weather across \nthe region, however there are a few periods where a few showers may \nmove into western NY. Beginning late Monday, a shortwave trough is \nexpected to approach the region from the southwest. While isentropic \nlift and deeper moisture is expected to stay south of the forecast \narea, mid-high level moisture will support a few showers across the \nSouthern Tier. A weak 850mb low is expected to develop over the Ohio \nValley and remain nearly stationary mid-week. An easterly flow off \nthe Atlantic will create cloudy conditions across western NY, with \nthe potential for scattered showers near the NY/PA border. Outside \nof the western Southern Tier, mostly dry weather is expected through \nFriday.",
